{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target asserts that the single mechanism best explaining cross-city variation in the nocturnal UHI gap is city size, via aggregation of thermal-mass volume. But the dominant established modulator of nocturnal UHI magnitude across cities is reduced longwave radiative cooling from restricted sky view factor in dense urban geometry, not absolute heat-storage volume. By committing to 'scales directly with city size', the target fails the problem's requirement to identify the mechanism that best explains why the night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others: a compact mid-size city with deep street canyons can exhibit a larger nocturnal UHI than a sprawling, low-density but physically larger city, directly contradicting the proposed direct scaling.","counterexample":["A physically large, low-density sprawling city (e.g., Phoenix-style suburban sprawl) with low building heights and high sky view factor can have a smaller nocturnal UHI than a geographically smaller but densely built city (e.g., central Tokyo or Manhattan) with deep street canyons and low sky view factor, contradicting the claim that the effect 'scales directly with city size'."],"premise":"The target identifies the single cross-city modulator as city size, via direct scaling of aggregate urban thermal-mass volume.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b90864a75220e09b302500bc","quote":"what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others"},{"block":"76e004fe088303108e2bb05d102aebec6a37de8475e909e4d716c82bcb7250bf","quote":"the cross-city magnitude of this effect scales directly with city size because larger cities contain a greater absolute volume of heat-storing masonry and asphalt"}],"target":"bedef52c8e2a6d51364b75c2dca7c96fdab2f193101df0b5a285c49984210805"}]}